<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bahadur Shah of Gujarat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1526">1526</a>–<a href="/years/1537">1537</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bahadur Shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> was sultan of Gujarat from 1526 to 1537. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and sacked Chittor, but was defeated by the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> in 1535. To win Portuguese help he ceded Bassein and allowed them to build a fort at Diu. In 1537 he was killed during a meeting on a Portuguese ship off Diu, and the sultanate declined.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-of-gujarat.jpg" alt="Bahadur Shah of Gujarat" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1586-1587 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Bahadur_fleeing_from_the_Fort_of_Cambay_in_1535.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Bahadur Khan into the Muzaffarid dynasty, he was the son of Prince Khalil Khan, who became sultan in 1511 as Muzaffar Shah II, and of Rani Lakshmi Bai, daughter of a Gohel Rajput. His grandfather, Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-begada">Mahmud Begada</a>, kept him at court. When his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-shah-bengal">Sikandar Shah</a> was named heir, Bahadur Khan feared for his life and fled, first to Chittor and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a>; he was present at the Battle of Panipat without taking part in the fighting.</p><p>Muzaffar Shah II died in 1526. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-shah-bengal">Sikandar Shah</a> was murdered after a few weeks on the instructions of his slave Imad-ul-Mulk Khush Kadam, who put an infant brother on the throne as Mahmud Shah II. Bahadur Khan returned, the nobles joined him, and he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champaner">Champaner</a>, executed Imad-ul-Mulk and had the child poisoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Bahadur Shah campaigned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> in 1528 and 1529, taking the fort of Ahmednagar and forcing the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> to sign a humiliating treaty. The fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a> fell on 28 March 1531, when Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-ii-of-malwa">Mahmud Shah II of Malwa</a> and his seven sons were captured; they were later put to death by their guards during a rescue attempt. In 1532 he raised a siege of Chittor in return for an enormous ransom. Until his defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> the power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> was at its height, with the rulers of Ahmednagar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanpur">Burhanpur</a> acknowledging him as overlord.</p><p>The quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> began because Bahadur Shah had sheltered the Mughal prince Muhammad Zaman Mirza. The rout near Mandasur is usually explained by the treachery of Rumi Khan, the Turkish head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> artillery. Bahadur Shah fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champaner">Champaner</a> and finally Diu. When Humayun left to face the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a>, the country rose, and Bahadur Shah defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> at Kanij and expelled them from Gujarat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Treaty of Bassein was signed on 23 December 1534 aboard the galleon St. Mattheus. Having recovered his kingdom, Bahadur Shah regretted the alliance. In February 1537 he visited the Portuguese viceroy's ship off Diu and was attacked and killed on his way back, on 13 February; his body was thrown into the sea. According to the Mirat-i-Sikandari, the viceroy had intercepted his message inviting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> sultans to ally against the Portuguese. He left no son, and after his chosen heir, his nephew Miran Muhammad Shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khandesh">Khandesh</a>, died on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, the nobles chose Mahmud Shah III, son of his brother Latif Khan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bahadur Shah of Gujarat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bahadur Shah of Gujarat</b> sacked Chittor in 1535, and in 1567–1568 Akbar besieged and took the fortress, which was defended by Jaimal and Patta while Udai Singh II withdrew to the hills.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bahadur Shah</b> ceded Bassein and allowed a fort at Diu to the Portuguese and was killed by them in 1537.</blockquote><a href="/state/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Gujarat Sultanate. Died in Diu, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.72,70.98&amp;year=1531" aria-label="Diu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6461 1397 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Bahadur Shah of Gujarat"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6911 1667h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6911 1667h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=20.72,70.98&amp;year=1531">What was here in 1531? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
